movie 12-plus hours a day for a month and you&nbsp;
do that for a year or so for free…how exactly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.320 --> 00:01:05.840
do you pay rent question?” Okay here's another&nbsp;
one “I’ve been working my tail on? I’m going…

00:01:05.840 --> 00:01:09.280
Shane: Probably tail off he doesn't think&nbsp;
he's working the tail off they're working&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.280 --> 00:01:16.160
their tail yeah wax on wax off sorry okay um&nbsp;
I’ve been working my tail off on crappy jobs&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:16.160 --> 00:01:22.160
outside the movie industry and I cannot accept&nbsp;
unpaid jobs inside the industry because I have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.160 --> 00:01:27.360
to eat and live and that costs money I am a&nbsp;
little confused about this attitude of Stanley&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.920 --> 00:01:34.400
thanks that you are supporting such a bad role&nbsp;
model I'm a bad role model okay god I feel bad&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.400 --> 00:01:40.640
that so much of this was misconstrued and I don't&nbsp;
blame the the the viewers and and I'd first like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.640 --> 00:01:44.720
to apologize if anything was misunderstood I'm not&nbsp;
telling you to sell your soul work for free live&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:44.720 --> 00:01:52.160
on the streets and not pay rent um let me clarify&nbsp;
a few things with that um because I encourage you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.160 --> 00:01:56.960
to get out there and work and hustle and do what&nbsp;
you have to do to get on a set a lot of the sets&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.960 --> 00:02:02.560
that you're going to get on cannot afford you&nbsp;
anyway and they or they are already filled and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.440 --> 00:02:10.000
what my point was it doesn't mean you go work&nbsp;
75 hours a week for free six days a week on a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:10.000 --> 00:02:16.640
film it means you make yourself available to a&nbsp;
producer or a filmmaker and say look I have a job&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.200 --> 00:02:24.000
I need to get time on a set can I put in two days&nbsp;
a week I’m off Saturday and Tuesdays I'm off the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.000 --> 00:02:31.120
weekend I'm off at night and get yourself involved&nbsp;
and in the loop of people that are doing what you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:31.120 --> 00:02:36.880
ultimately want to do I'm not saying that you've&nbsp;
got to go you know live in your car to go work for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.880 --> 00:02:43.200
free I personally worked for free a lot but I also&nbsp;
had other jobs just because I had a father in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.200 --> 00:02:48.960
industry trust me it didn't help me in a way that&nbsp;
a lot of people think it might have when I when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.960 --> 00:02:55.280
I decided that I wanted to make a living in film&nbsp;
the only thing my father gave me was his rolodex&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.280 --> 00:03:01.200
he said great there you go you know some of the&nbsp;
people in there make some calls I worked for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.200 --> 00:03:06.960
hundreds of different people before he hired&nbsp;
me full-time I wasn't until my mid-20s until I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.960 --> 00:03:11.520
was working with my dad full-time we had a lot of&nbsp;
success early on with his documentaries and shows&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:11.520 --> 00:03:18.240
that he did we did one every two years I moved&nbsp;
out when I was 18 I didn't have my rent covered&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:18.240 --> 00:03:24.720
I was out hustling I held odd jobs I sold cars I&nbsp;
worked at restaurants I waited tables I bus tables&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:24.720 --> 00:03:29.200
you know I detailed cars I mowed lawns&nbsp;
I painted houses I did construction&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:36.080
I still found a way to get on a set every week&nbsp;
and put in my time to make new contacts so what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:36.080 --> 00:03:42.160
I'm trying to encourage you guys to understand&nbsp;
is it's not going to come to you you have to make&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.160 --> 00:03:48.560
sacrifices I am not asking you to yourself out and&nbsp;
work for free for everybody all the time to start&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:48.560 --> 00:03:54.640
to get a track record as a reliable resource on a&nbsp;
set it's like interning you know we have a lot of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:54.640 --> 00:04:00.320
interns that come and go on our shows from films&nbsp;
from film school that work on our productions we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:00.320 --> 00:04:06.400
we get them when we can they get credit some&nbsp;
of our greatest regular crew members that we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:06.400 --> 00:04:11.040
pay are from interns from college that&nbsp;
came in and worked for two or three days&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:11.040 --> 00:04:16.960
a week if we were lucky during a production from&nbsp;
four years ago it's just to encourage everybody&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:16.960 --> 00:04:22.560
to understand it's not coming to you if it's not&nbsp;
coming to you how are you going to get something&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:22.560 --> 00:04:28.240
you know if you want to get to an island it ain't&nbsp;
coming to you you either swim or take a boat if&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:28.240 --> 00:04:32.800
you don't have money for a boat you don't have the&nbsp;
resources for a boat get in water start swimming&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:32.800 --> 00:04:37.120
that's all I'm trying to say it's to encourage&nbsp;
you guys to understand that's what I had to do&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:37.120 --> 00:04:41.840
I brought up a friend of mine whose father had two&nbsp;
oscars his mother had I think five or seven Emmys&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:42.480 --> 00:04:46.560
when he pulled his head out of his behind and&nbsp;
decided he wanted to work in the film industry&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:46.560 --> 00:04:51.840
he told his parents his plan assuming they would&nbsp;
get him work they said great dad picked up the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:51.840 --> 00:04:56.960
phone and I think he called deluxe which&nbsp;
was an old film color house back in the day&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:58.080 --> 00:05:02.720
my friend was sweeping floors there for two&nbsp;
years before they let him sit behind an edit bay&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:03.440 --> 00:05:08.160
that was the job he got the first year he was&nbsp;
there he was there two or three hours a day&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:08.160 --> 00:05:13.280
and he wasn't paid that's just how it was back in&nbsp;
our day if I offend people of today's generation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:13.280 --> 00:05:18.400
because we actually worked for free and earned our&nbsp;
stripes by busting ass and bringing our own lunch&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:18.400 --> 00:05:24.400
sorry you were offended but that's how we did it&nbsp;
we still have people that want to intern that show&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:24.400 --> 00:05:28.560
up we still have great people that come in and&nbsp;
say look I'd like to give you a day on the set I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:28.560 --> 00:05:33.440
want to show you what I'm about because we're not&nbsp;
looking for the help we have the people that are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:33.440 --> 00:05:38.480
on the team so it's about people coming to us and&nbsp;
saying I want to show you what I can do for you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:38.480 --> 00:05:42.480
of course we're going to feed you we'll water&nbsp;
you come on out for a few hours show us what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:42.480 --> 00:05:47.040
you got so when we make our next film we know to&nbsp;
call you and there's a lot of filmmakers that are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:47.040 --> 00:05:52.160
willing to do that I used to call everybody I&nbsp;
knew in that rolodex and I called them and we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:52.160 --> 00:05:58.000
didn't have text and email it was phone calls and&nbsp;
it was you know hey Phil Hearn it's Shane Stanley,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:58.000 --> 00:06:05.520
Lee's son. Yeah that one. Listen I need to get on&nbsp;
a set do you need somebody to be a PA, an AC, a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:05.520 --> 00:06:10.400
second you know what can I do to get on a set Phil&nbsp;
would call back sometimes and say yeah if you want&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:10.400 --> 00:06:15.600
to come work on Saturday we're doing a show with&nbsp;
Richard Crenna come on out bring your own lunch&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:15.600 --> 00:06:21.200
I’ll get you on the set giant getting paid and I'd&nbsp;
show up you know I did that with Steve Elkins Phil&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:21.200 --> 00:06:28.000
Hearn did that with Ken Shafer I did that with&nbsp;
with Tom Coster I did that with with you know chad&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:28.000 --> 00:06:34.480
shoe cat I think got me on a couple of shows it&nbsp;
it's just what we did and I hope and I understand&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:34.480 --> 00:06:40.560
times have changed and all that but my my heart&nbsp;
and my spirit of telling you what I did last&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:40.560 --> 00:06:46.800
time that ruffled some feathers was not sell your&nbsp;
soul and work for free for 70 hours a week do your&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:47.520 --> 00:06:53.200
meet your responsibilities with your paying job&nbsp;
but we all don't work 70 hours a week for a paying&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:53.200 --> 00:06:59.600
job so you find the time to get on a set even if&nbsp;
it's three hours a day and show people what you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:59.600 --> 00:07:05.280
got and be around people that are doing what you&nbsp;
love so they know that they can call and rely on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:05.280 --> 00:07:12.800
you that was the sole purpose of that why do you&nbsp;
think um younger generations have reacted stronger&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:12.800 --> 00:07:18.320
than maybe you know previous ones it's because&nbsp;
a lot of interns have been taken advantage of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:18.320 --> 00:07:23.200
yeah I think so I mean when you work with&nbsp;
interns you can't you can't replace interns&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:23.200 --> 00:07:27.040
you can't replace employees with interns and&nbsp;
I think you know ever since the big Black Swan&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:28.160 --> 00:07:32.960
that changed the dynamics you know we work very&nbsp;
closely with a lot of amazing schools you know the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:32.960 --> 00:07:38.080
LACC [Los Angeles Community College] we're working&nbsp;
with College of the Desert this year out in Palm&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:38.080 --> 00:07:44.080
Springs and Pierce College and you know the&nbsp;
rules are simple is is you know give the kids&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:44.080 --> 00:07:50.400
the opportunity let them shadow and and our our&nbsp;
guys on our crew are so amazing with them because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:50.400 --> 00:07:55.280
they they've been fortunate in their upbringing&nbsp;
and they understand what hard work is they don't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:55.280 --> 00:07:58.800
let the kids sit around they're like hey grab an&nbsp;
end of that come on let me show you how to clean&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:58.800 --> 00:08:04.960
a lens when we had interns literally doing second&nbsp;
AC duties for for a film when we had a second AD&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:05.520 --> 00:08:11.200
I'm sorry second AC and and to watch these kids&nbsp;
learn how to pick up these 60 or 70 thousand&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:11.200 --> 00:08:15.280
dollar lenses and clean them and prep them and put&nbsp;
them on the cameras they're not learning that in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:15.280 --> 00:08:18.960
the classroom and I guarantee you they're not&nbsp;
learning that during a pandemic so we're really&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:18.960 --> 00:08:24.800
big about interns coming out and learning various&nbsp;
jobs on the set you know we try to move them&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:24.800 --> 00:08:31.280
to sound to camera to AD, UPM they come shadow me&nbsp;
for a day they can learn everything what not to do&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:32.160 --> 00:08:36.480
you know go in a hair and makeup learn how that&nbsp;
is go learn wardrobe and how we tag everything&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:36.480 --> 00:08:40.800
for the days and the scenes and how sometimes&nbsp;
wardrobe is used for half of the day it's put&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:40.800 --> 00:08:44.800
away we use it for an hour something else and&nbsp;
then we go back to that and just the way the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:44.800 --> 00:08:50.240
schedule is how we shoot they don't learn&nbsp;
these things in a classroom and you know&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:52.320 --> 00:08:57.200
they're paying for an education that that they get&nbsp;
in a class and so when they're able to come on a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:57.200 --> 00:09:02.080
set with filmmakers that are willing to open their&nbsp;
doors and their hearts to say come learn you know&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:02.080 --> 00:09:21.840
and that's that's what that's that's the spirit&nbsp;
of what I what I try to to you know to encourage.

